--TEST--
a `Core\Router::url` `$params` key that names no capture becomes a percent-encoded query string, spelled exactly as `Core\Uri::buildQuery` spells it — `rule:routing/an-absolute-link-takes-a-configured-origin`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's other half. The prepared path names every capture, so a key still in
// `$params` once they have been substituted is by construction not one, and
// § 6 makes it a query parameter rather than an error. Every such key is
// declared with `#[Query]` below, because the refusal half of the same sentence
// is now applied: a key that is neither a capture nor a declared `#[Query]`
// parameter is `E0759`, and that is what keeps a typo from silently shipping as
// a query string. The declared *type* is what a query value arrives at (§ 3),
// and it says nothing about what a link may be handed — `tag` below is written
// with an array and still encodes.
class Posts {
    // `rule:attributes/access-is-a-required-sibling`: the decision is a required sibling of every `#[Route]`, and
    // `Core\Audience::Public` is § 1a's case for a route that is genuinely open.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{id}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(
        uint $id,
        #[Core\Query] string $sort = "",
        #[Core\Query] string $q = "",
        #[Core\Query] string $note = "",
        #[Core\Query] string $tag = "",
    ): string { return "post " . $id; }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{page?}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::list")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function list(uint $page = 1, #[Core\Query] string $sort = ""): string { return "page " . $page; }
}

// The complement, asserted on both sides of one call: `id` is the capture and
// is spent on the path, `sort` is not and is spent on the query. A pass that
// wrote every key would print `?id=7&sort=new`.
echo "split=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7, "sort" => "new"]), "\n";

// Nothing left over is no `?` at all, rather than an empty one — the two are
// different URLs to a cache and to a signature.
echo "none=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7]), "\n";

// An optional capture nobody filled drops its whole segment (`rule:routing/path-grammar`) and
// the query is still written: the two halves of the path do not interact.
echo "optional=", Core\Router::url("Posts::list", ["sort" => "new"]), "\n";

// Entry order, `&`-joined, and every byte outside the unreserved set escaped
// under `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` — a space is `+`, and `/`, `&` and
// `=` cannot close the value they are written inside.
echo "escaped=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7, "q" => "a/b&c=d", "note" => "two words"]), "\n";

// The query half is `Core\Uri::buildQuery`'s own pass rather than a second
// convention, so the agreement below is what pins it: the same leftover keys,
// asked of both members, answer the same text. A link that grew its own
// encoder would still look plausible on the line above and fail here. The
// answer prints as `1` because that is what `true as string` is (`rule:types/conversion`).
string $query = Core\Uri::buildQuery(["q" => "a/b&c=d", "note" => "two words"]);
echo "agrees=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7, "q" => "a/b&c=d", "note" => "two words"]) == "/posts/7?" . $query, "\n";

// And the conventions that come with it: a nested value goes under its whole
// bracketed path with the brackets themselves encoded, and a `null` is dropped
// rather than written as an empty value — `http_build_query`'s two rules, held
// here because there is one implementation and not two.
echo "nested=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7, "tag" => ["a" => 1, "b" => 2]]), "\n";
echo "null=", Core\Router::url("Posts::show", ["id" => 7, "sort" => null]), "\n";
--EXPECT--
split=/posts/7?sort=new
none=/posts/7
optional=/posts?sort=new
escaped=/posts/7?q=a%2Fb%26c%3Dd&note=two+words
agrees=1
nested=/posts/7?tag%5Ba%5D=1&tag%5Bb%5D=2
null=/posts/7
